{"v": 1, "ausgabe": 10, "datum": "2026-11-23", "titel": "", "erstellt": "2026-11-23T08:52:00+01:00", "abgeglichen": "2026-11-23T08:52:04+01:00", "wetter": "Salzburg 0 to 3 °C, light rain, 95 % chance of showers", "sonne": "Sunrise 7:24", "kurz": ["Consulting Deep Dive, part 8: commercial due diligence, the fastest lane from strategy work into private equity", "Kellogg's new MBA class: 528 students, a median GMAT Focus of 685 and a record share of STEM graduates", "Mira Murati's Thinking Machines puts a capable open model on a laptop with Inkling 2 Mini"], "anzahlMeldungen": 4, "ressorts": [{"name": "Consulting", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Consulting Deep Dive, part 8: commercial due diligence, the fastest lane from strategy work into private equity", "text": "Commercial due diligence (CDD) is the work a buyer commissions before signing a deal: is the market as large and as fast-growing as the seller claims, are the customers loyal, can the target defend its margins, and does the investment thesis survive contact with reality? For private equity funds, which buy companies largely with borrowed money and must sell them again within a few years, that answer is worth paying for under extreme time pressure. A whole tier of the consulting industry has organised itself around it.\n\nBain is the reference point. Its private equity practice says it has run more than 22,000 due diligence cases since 2000 and advised on over half of all scale buyout transactions in that period, combining strategic, commercial, operational and technology questions. L.E.K. Consulting presents CDD as a core service alongside vendor and operational due diligence and portfolio value creation, and says it works on more than 400 projects a year for private equity clients; one company page puts its total at roughly 4,000 transactions over the past decade. OC&C, a boutique strong in consumer, retail and business services, is described as a diligence specialist with outsized deal flow; one of its partners has said that about half of the firm's work is for private equity, from pre-deal diligence to value creation after the purchase. EY-Parthenon, Strategy& Deals and smaller houses such as CIL also live largely off buy-side and sell-side diligence, while Roland Berger is regarded as the European heavyweight for industrial and automotive deals.\n\nWhat does the work feel like? Expect market modelling, a great deal of primary research (customer interviews, surveys, expert calls) and relentless pressure-testing of the investment thesis, usually on short, intense projects whose deliverable is a view the fund can defend before its investment committee. At EY-Parthenon, consultants typically start as generalists and only later align with industries, which means exposure to many sectors in quick succession.\n\nThe exit logic is straightforward. Private equity is the most common exit from L.E.K., and the one its work is built for. Two years of CDD teach you to think like an investor: which markets deserve a premium multiple, how funds frame value creation plans, and you meet deal teams across the table. The gap, as career guides point out, is technical: CDD firms build commercial cases, not leveraged buyout models, so candidates usually have to teach themselves LBO mechanics before recruiting for associate roles at funds. Corporate development and M&A teams inside companies, and corporate strategy, are the other frequent destinations. Industry voices also note that the market has become harder: with exits from fund portfolios slower, more consulting work has shifted from pure pre-deal diligence towards value creation in companies funds already own.\n\nThe trade-off against generalist strategy work is breadth. CDD-heavy firms give you deep investor exposure early, but fewer long transformation projects inside large corporates; MBB, and Bain in particular, offer both but are harder to get into. A sensible sequence for a would-be investor is therefore Bain or a CDD specialist for two to three years, deliberate exposure to private equity cases, then a move to a fund or to corporate development.", "url": "https://www.bain.com/industry-expertise/private-equity/", "quelle": "Bain, Private Equity", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@db7fa40f7b2d19d6de0c6c399737310327fcbd3b/cdc3f14c71279c37.mp3"}]}, {"name": "Business Schools and Master's", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Kellogg's new MBA class: 528 students, a median GMAT Focus of 685 and a record share of STEM graduates", "text": "Northwestern University's Kellogg School of Management has published the profile of its Two-Year MBA Class of 2028, and it says a good deal about where elite business education is heading. The school enrolled 528 students this autumn. A record 45 per cent majored in science, technology, engineering or mathematics (some students count twice because of double majors). The median undergraduate GPA among graduates of US institutions is 3.72 on a 4.0 scale.\n\nThe headline number for anyone preparing applications is the test score: the class has a median GMAT Focus score of 685. Poets and Quants, which analysed the profile, reports that the international share fell to 35 per cent; Kellogg's Class of 2026 was still 40 per cent international. Poets and Quants also highlights a more generous GI Bill benefit for military veterans, a group American schools court actively.\n\nBoth trends are long-running. Business schools increasingly recruit engineers and quantitative graduates, whom employers in consulting and technology prize, and STEM-designated programmes in the US give international graduates longer post-study work permission. The falling international share, by contrast, suggests that foreign candidates have become more cautious about American programmes.\n\nFor European pre-experience programmes such as the Master in Management, the read-across is indirect: if fewer internationals head to American schools, more of that talent may stay in Europe, which could stiffen competition at HEC, LBS or INSEAD in coming cycles.", "url": "https://poetsandquants.com/2026/11/22/kelloggs-mba-class-of-2028-more-stem-fewer-internationals-and-a-bigger-gi-bill-benefit-for-veterans/?pq-category=class-profiles-demographics", "quelle": "Poets and Quants", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@db7fa40f7b2d19d6de0c6c399737310327fcbd3b/02d93097fb649de9.mp3"}]}, {"name": "AI and Claude", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Mira Murati's Thinking Machines puts a capable open model on a laptop with Inkling 2 Mini", "text": "Thinking Machines, the AI start-up founded by OpenAI's former chief technology officer Mira Murati, has released Inkling 2 Mini, an open-weights model small enough to run on a MacBook. In the independent ranking by Artificial Analysis it matches Alibaba's considerably larger Qwen3.8 27B. It arrives just two weeks after its bigger sibling, Inkling 2 Small.\n\nThe trick is architectural. Inkling 2 Mini is a \"looped transformer\": of its 24 layers, the middle 12 run three times for every token, buying more computation per token without a bigger model. It stores 26.4 billion parameters but activates only 3.4 billion per token (5.4 billion counting the loops), handles images as well as text and, according to Artificial Analysis, offers a context window of one million tokens. It needs about 20 gigabytes of memory and is meant for any Mac with at least 32 gigabytes; the company demonstrated it on a MacBook Pro in airplane mode. The weights are on Hugging Face under the permissive Apache 2.0 licence, and through the company's API it costs 0.15 dollars per million input tokens and 0.50 dollars per million output tokens.\n\nEfficiency is the selling point. A task from the Artificial Analysis index costs 0.59 dollars and takes nine minutes, against 3.53 dollars and 38 minutes for Qwen: roughly six times cheaper and four times faster. The model is particularly strong at coding and agentic tool use, and because it runs locally, sensitive data never leaves the device.\n\nThe caveats are candid. Thinking Machines concedes it is \"not the strongest small model\" for long chats or tightly constrained instructions. Knowledge depends on total parameters, which the loops do not add; it hallucinates more than Qwen (a non-hallucination rate of 63.4 against 71.4 per cent) and is unusually verbose. Nearly all benchmarks come from the developer, and the looped design raises a safety question: when a model reasons internally several times without writing it out, its \"thinking\" is harder to monitor.", "url": "https://www.trendingtopics.eu/inkling-2-mini-thinking-machines-laptop-en/", "quelle": "Trending Topics", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@db7fa40f7b2d19d6de0c6c399737310327fcbd3b/049be583c8b5e773.mp3"}]}, {"name": "Policy Check", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Brussels' budget broker trims the next EU budget and revives a tax on Big Tech", "text": "The decision. European Council President António Costa has tabled a revised negotiating box for the EU's next seven-year budget, the multiannual financial framework (MFF) for 2028 to 2034, ahead of a special summit on 26 and 27 November. According to several reports over the weekend, it would cut about 180.5 billion euros from the Commission's original plan and add a 3 per cent digital services tax on large technology companies as a new revenue source for the EU. Any such tax requires the unanimous consent of all 27 member states; if the November summit fails, the next opportunity is the European Council on 17 and 18 December.\n\nThe case made. Costa's argument, carried to capitals on his tour before the summit, is that there is no deal without new EU-level revenue (\"own resources\"), because otherwise every euro of new spending on defence or competitiveness must come from national contributions. Critics attack from two sides: net payers want a smaller budget, while a tax aimed mostly at American firms risks retaliation from Washington. The Commission itself dropped a digital levy from its July 2025 proposal, and France is reported to have rejected the compromise. Update, Monday morning: according to Der Standard, the revised plan is meeting broad resistance from governments, the European Parliament, farmers and regions alike, so the dispute runs on into the summit.\n\nThe numbers. The Commission's July 2025 proposal amounted to 1,984 billion euros in current prices, or 1.26 per cent of EU gross national income (GNI), including 0.11 per cent for repaying NextGenerationEU debt (European Parliament Research Service, EPRS). Excluding that repayment it equals 1.15 per cent of GNI, against 1.13 per cent when the current 2021 to 2027 budget was agreed; inflation has since eroded the current framework to about 1.02 per cent (EPRS, 2026). In 2025 prices the Commission asked for 1,763 billion euros in commitments; the Irish presidency's October draft cut that to 1,622 billion, about 141 billion or 8 per cent less. The Commission's five proposed new own resources were to raise 58.5 billion euros a year, and a European Parliament package of digital, gambling and crypto taxes was estimated at up to 25 billion euros a year.\n\nReality check. The claim that the EU budget leans ever more heavily on national GNI contributions is supported by the Commission's own figures and EPRS analyses. The reported cut points the same way as the Irish draft, only further. What remains unsupported is any published estimate of what a 3 per cent digital tax would actually yield, how it would interact with national digital taxes already in force, or how large the risk of US retaliation is. Whether the EU should spend more or less is a value judgement the data cannot settle, and the exact baseline of the 180.5 billion figure (current or constant prices) could not be verified.\n\nEvidence rating: Thinly grounded. The underlying funding gap is well documented, but the specific remedy, a 3 per cent levy on Big Tech, has been put forward without a public estimate of its revenue or its economic cost.", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMic0FVX3lxTE1yaXIwQkV6cElINVUxaC15YXI1WVVIS3FQZlpmV2dSdGVQS2pTeW84blJuZ0VfMnMta1p4dW1HUnQtbU1QRVlsZXNzTjVPSVBabTlMT3J2cURFUjBKQk5JZEZ2MU1GVUhnVVNGNmpJdkV2RlU?oc=5", "quelle": "CEO Magazyn"}]}], "fuss": "Created on 23 November 2026 at 07:30 by Claude. Last updated 08:52. Sources linked under each report. Gmail could not be checked; the connector needs a fresh sign-in. Outlook brought one new reply this morning (Deutsche Rentenversicherung), nothing further since.", "privat": {"iv": "5rH1f9c0/C/po49WrObcmQ==", "ct": "", "mac": "7kgDAa4gzwNSwEOaG8YVIhDmvy6SgNBXYGiikK94FRI="}, "archiv": [{"ausgabe": 10, "datum": "2026-11-23", "titel": ""}, {"ausgabe": 9, "datum": "2026-11-22", "titel": ""}, {"ausgabe": 8, "datum": "2026-11-21", "titel": ""}, {"ausgabe": 7, "datum": "2026-11-20", "titel": ""}, {"ausgabe": 6, "datum": "2026-11-19", "titel": ""}, {"ausgabe": 5, "datum": "2026-11-18", "titel": ""}, {"ausgabe": 4, "datum": "2026-11-17", "titel": ""}]}